          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What HbA1c target does guidance describe, and is there one number everybody uses?",
          "answer": "There is not one number that every guideline uses. The retrieved guidance describes a general target and a range of individualized targets, and the sources disagree on how to present them.\n\n**The general target.** Diabetes association guidance (the National diabetes association standards of care summary) describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. The aim is to reduce small-blood-vessel complications affecting the eyes, kidneys and nerves. That guidance also says the evidence that tighter glucose control alone protects large blood vessels is weaker, so glucose control sits alongside blood pressure and cholesterol management rather than replacing them.\n\n**Individualized targets.** The same guidance says the target should be individualized. A stricter goal, such as below 6.5 percent, may suit people who can reach it without significant low blood sugar, who have had diabetes for a shorter time, and who have a longer life expectancy. Severe low blood sugar is a reason to relax the goal. The retrieved passages did not give a separate numeric target for older adults or for people with limited life expectancy, so I can't report one.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** Guidance differs on how firmly to state a single number. Some guidance uses 7 percent as the default and adjusts it for each person. Other guidance treats the individualized range as the main approach and does not give a headline figure, because it worries that a single number gets applied more rigidly than intended.\n\n**Two related numbers.** The 6.5 percent figure in the retrieved material also appears as a diagnostic threshold. An HbA1c at or above 6.5 percent is one of the criteria for diagnosing diabetes. That is a different question from the treatment goal.\n\n**What I could not check.** The retrieved material did not include guidance from named organizations such as NICE (UK) or the IDF, so I cannot compare their targets. Ask a clinician which target fits your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
